\date{2026-08-18T14:22:54Z}
\import{base-macros}
\author{kellenkanarios}
\title{The backward pass is two matmuls, not one}
\taxon{Card}
\tag{card}
\meta{anki}{1787063264071}
\meta{anki-deck}{MLSys}

\prompt{One matmul #{C = AB} with #{A[N,P]}, #{B[P,M]} costs #{2NPM} FLOPs at inference but #{6NPM} in training. Where do the other two thirds come from?}{
  The forward pass contracts over #{P} and costs #{2NPM}. [[SGKK]] then needs \em{two} matmuls of exactly that size, not one:

  \ul{
    \li{#{\partial L/\partial B = A^{\top}(\partial L/\partial C)}, contracting over #{N} — the parameter gradient;}
    \li{#{\partial L/\partial A = (\partial L/\partial C)B^{\top}}, contracting over #{M} — not a parameter gradient at all, but the signal handed to earlier layers.}
  }

  Each of the three contracts a different axis of the same #{N \times P \times M} box, so each costs #{2NPM} and the total is #{6NPM}, three times the forward pass. It is easy to remember only the first backward matmul and land on #{4NPM}; the activation gradient is the one that goes missing.

  \strong{Why the constant is worth memorising.} #{PM} is the parameter count of that matrix and #{N} is the token count, so #{6NPM} is precisely the #{6 \cdot \mathrm{params} \cdot \mathrm{tokens}} rule of thumb — the whole training cost of a [[YB7M]] is a sum of such terms.
}
